  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Read problems statements in Russian.Chef has an array AAA of size NNN. Chef wants to choose any subsequence of size **exactly** ⌈N2⌉ \\lceil \\frac{N}{2} \\rceil⌈2N​⌉ from the array such that GCD of all the elements in that sequence must be 222. Chef names such a kind of sequence as a half-sequence.\n\nHelp Chef to find whether he would be able to select any half-sequence in the given array.\n\nAs a reminder,\n\n\nA subsequence of an array is a sequence that can be derived from the given array by deleting zero or more elements without changing the order of the remaining elements.\nGCD stands for Greatest Common Divisor. The greatest common divisor of a subsequence is the largest integer ddd such that all the numbers in sequence are divisible by ddd. For more information, refer to here.\n⌈x⌉\\lceil x \\rceil⌈x⌉ is the ceiling (round up) operation: ⌈3.5⌉=4,⌈2⌉=2\\lceil 3.5 \\rceil = 4 ,\\lceil 2 \\rceil = 2⌈3.5⌉=4,⌈2⌉=2.\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains an integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The TTT test cases then follow.\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN denoting the size of the array.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2....ANA_1, A_2....A_NA1​,A2​....AN​ denoting the given array.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on one line `YES` if Chef can find a half-sequence, else print `NO`. Output is case insensitive.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤201 \\le T \\le 201≤T≤20",
      "2≤N≤1052 \\le N \\le 10^52≤N≤105",
      "1≤Ai≤1091 \\le A_i \\le 10^91≤Ai​≤109",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10510^5105"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n5\n1 2 3 4 5\n4\n1 2 3 4\n3\n30 42 70",
        "output": "NO\nYES\nNO",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nFor the first test case, Chef wants to select ⌈52⌉=3\\lceil \\frac{5}{2} \\rceil = 3⌈25​⌉=3 numbers. But for any 333 numbers, GCD would not be 222. Therefore the answer is NO.\n\n\nFor the second test case, Chef wants to select ⌈42⌉=2\\lceil \\frac{4}{2} \\rceil = 2⌈24​⌉=2 numbers. Chef can select the subsequence [2,4][2,4][2,4] with GCD of 222. Therefore the answer is YES.\n\n\nFor the third test case, Chef wants to select ⌈32⌉=2\\lceil \\frac{3}{2} \\rceil = 2⌈23​⌉=2 numbers. But for any 222 numbers, GCD would be bigger than 222. Therefore the answer is NO."
      }
    ]
  },
